;
; strobe_capture — one state machine: timestamp and latch the 16-line event code.
;
; REFERENCE LISTING ONLY — NOT ASSEMBLED, AND NOTHING READS THIS FILE.
; piolib exposes pio_encode_*, so the program is built as instruction words in
; wl_sync/rp1/program.py and handed over as a plain uint16_t*. No pioasm, no build
; step. This file is the annotated source form of what that module encodes; keep
; the two in step by eye, and trust program.py, which has tests.
;
; DRAFTED 2026-08-22 WITHOUT HARDWARE. Task 5b's bench acceptance (±100 µs over a
; 10-minute run, zero dropped words) has not been run. Every cycle count below is
; reasoned from the RP2040 PIO instruction set, which RP1's PIO is documented as
; being compatible with, and NONE of it has been assembled or executed. The
; "Unverified" list at the bottom is what to check first on bench day.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IT PUSHES: two words per event, not one.
;
;   word 1   the 16 captured data lines, upper 16 bits zero
;   word 2   the raw down-counter; the host inverts it (wl_sync.rp1.pio_capture)
;
; One word carrying data plus a tick DELTA was the earlier design and could not
; work: 16 data lines leave 16 delta bits = 65.535 ms, while trials are ~3 s, so
; ordinary inter-trial gaps overflowed the field silently. Covering 3 s in 16 bits
; needs ≥45.8 µs per tick, which spends half the ±100 µs budget on quantisation
; before anything else goes wrong. A full 32-bit tick wraps at 71.6 minutes —
; the same period as now_us(), so the host already knows how to unwrap it.
;
; The upper 16 bits of word 1 being zero is what makes a dropped word DETECTABLE
; rather than a permanent silent swap of data and time. See pair_fifo.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THE COUNTER RUNS DOWN: `jmp x--` is PIO's only decrement. There is no add
; and no subtract, so a free-running counter counts down from 0xFFFFFFFF and the
; host inverts. Inverting costs one host instruction and zero PIO instructions,
; and PIO instructions are the scarce resource.
;
; WHY EVERY PATH COSTS TWO CYCLES PER DECREMENT — this is the whole design.
; A naive version counts only in the idle loop, so every capture and every
; strobe-high period is time the counter never saw. That loss is per-event and
; cumulative: at ~600 events in a 10-minute run even a 2 µs slip per event is
; 1.2 ms, which fails the ±100 µs gate on its own. So the capture path pads with
; `jmp x--` so that it too advances the counter once per two cycles, and the
; strobe-high wait is a counting loop rather than a `wait` instruction.
;
; `wait 0 pin` is deliberately NOT used anywhere: it stalls the state machine,
; and a stalled state machine is a stopped clock.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; CONFIGURATION REQUIRED OF THE HOST
;   in_base      = CODE_DATA_BASE (GPIO0), 16 contiguous lines — wl_sync.pins
;   jmp_pin      = CODE_STROBE_PIN (GPIO16)
;   in_shiftdir  = LEFT, so `in pins, 16` lands the lines in ISR bits 0..15 and
;                  leaves the upper half clear. Shifting right would put them at
;                  the top and destroy the pairing check.
;   autopush     = OFF; both pushes are explicit
;   clock        = 2 MHz, i.e. clkdiv = f_sys / 2e6, so the two-cycle loop
;                  advances the counter once per microsecond
;   X            = 0xFFFFFFFF at start
;

.program strobe_capture

.wrap_target
idle:
    jmp pin, capture            ; [1] strobe high → event
    jmp x--, idle               ; [1] tick, and keep waiting     = 2 cycles / tick
.wrap

capture:
    in pins, 16                 ; [1] latch the code word
    jmp x--, c_push_data        ; [1]                            = 2 cycles / tick
c_push_data:
    push noblock                ; [1] word 1: data
    jmp x--, c_load_counter     ; [1]                            = 2 cycles / tick
c_load_counter:
    mov isr, x                  ; [1] snapshot the counter
    jmp x--, c_push_counter     ; [1]                            = 2 cycles / tick
c_push_counter:
    push noblock                ; [1] word 2: raw counter
    jmp x--, held               ; [1]                            = 2 cycles / tick

; The strobe is still high. Count through it rather than waiting on it, so a wide
; strobe costs precision nothing. Re-arming only once it falls is what makes one
; strobe produce one capture.
held:
    jmp pin, held_tick          ; [1] still high?
    jmp idle                    ; [1] fallen → re-arm
held_tick:
    jmp x--, held               ; [1] tick while it stays high   = 2 cycles / tick

; 11 instructions of the 32-instruction memory, leaving 21 for edge capture.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; KNOWN, CONSTANT BIAS: `mov isr, x` runs 5 cycles after the strobe was observed,
; so every timestamp is ~2.5 µs late by a FIXED amount. Constant is the important
; word — it is identical for every event, so it is a host-side subtraction, not a
; jitter term. It must be measured on the bench rather than trusted from this
; comment, because it depends on the assembler's final instruction placement.
;
; UNVERIFIED — check these first on bench day, in this order:
;   1. `jmp x--` semantics at X = 0: this assumes it decrements to 0xFFFFFFFF and
;      falls through, and that `.wrap` then returns to `idle` with no lost cycle.
;      If it costs a cycle, the counter gains 0.5 µs once per 71.6 min — harmless,
;      but confirm rather than assume.
;   2. That `jmp x--, <next instruction>` is a legal way to spend a decrement, and
;      that it is one cycle. The entire ±100 µs argument rests on it.
;   3. `mov isr, x` ignoring in_shiftdir and copying all 32 bits straight.
;   4. RESOLVED 2026-08-22 against piolib's published header: there is no assembler
;      in the path at all, and Rp1Backend._assert_encoders_agree now checks every
;      instruction here against piolib's own pio_encode_* at startup, so an RP1/RP2040
;      divergence stops the box rather than corrupting timestamps. Items 1-3 remain
;      open because they are about SEMANTICS at runtime, which no encoder check sees.
;   5. `push noblock` DROPS on a full FIFO. With events seconds apart and an 8-deep
;      FIFO this should never fire, and pair_fifo will raise DesyncError if a single
;      word is lost — but a whole pair lost is invisible, so the bench run must
;      check the FIFO overflow flag directly to satisfy "zero dropped words".
